    love_attachment: "Attachment in Love",
    love_choice: "Choice in Love",
    love_intro: "Why do some people remain fiercely loyal in love, even to the point of dying for it, while others are recklessly promiscuous and have never truly loved? Why, when love brings such pain, do people flock to it anyway?",
    love_loss: "The Death of Love",
    love_mechanism: "The Mechanism of Love—Hormones, Nerves, and Positive Feedback",
    love_origin: "The Origin of Love: Evolution—All for Survival and Continuity",
    love_p0: "Over millions of years of survival and reproduction, genes favoring survival were retained while carriers of disadvantageous genes perished. Through continuous evolution, the neurons of romantic love first emerged in human ancestors about 4 million years ago. At that time, love’s function was to increase reproductive success, provide stable nurturing environments for offspring, promote cooperation and group cohesion, and enhance survival fitness. Yet, if that were all, how could love shape such richly diverse forms in modern life?",
    love_p1: "When we are with our beloved, dopamine-rich areas of the brain activate—regions linked to pleasure, arousal, focused attention, and reward-seeking motivation. Oxytocin and vasopressin rise, reducing anxiety, enhancing trust, inhibiting betrayal, and strengthening intimate contact.",
    love_p2: "When people are seen, respected, favored, and accepted by their beloved, dopamine, oxytocin, and vasopressin all rise, creating feelings of joy, safety, calm, and happiness—driving them toward more intimate contact and continually reinforcing the romantic relationship. But do not forget: the brain has a natural homeostatic counterbalance. After these positive events, it will reverse-regulate, generating negative emotions to restore equilibrium.",
    love_p3: "Romantic love shares the same reward pathways as addictive behaviors. The dopamine rush creates euphoria; when the lover is absent, craving and withdrawal-like anxiety set in—driving reunion-seeking behavior. This is why early romantic love resembles “addiction.”",
    love_p4: "In the early stages of love, serotonin drops significantly, reducing impulse control and intensifying obsessive thoughts about the beloved—even interfering with normal eating and sleep.",
    love_p5: "As attachment deepens, oxytocin and vasopressin take over, bringing calm, trust, and long-term bonding—transitioning from passionate love to companionate love.",
    love_p6: "Long-term relationships also recruit the anterior cingulate cortex and other empathy regions, enabling partners to genuinely share each other’s pain and joy—the neural basis for “two becoming one.”",
    love_p7: "Love, from its evolutionary origin, is a mechanism for survival and continuity; from its neural foundation, it is a chemical cascade of reward, attachment, and homeostatic counterbalance; from its phenomenological experience, it is a complex mix of euphoria, pain, obsession, calm, choice, and commitment.",
    love_p8: "Understanding this, we can better grasp why love is at once the most beautiful and the most painful of human experiences—and why, for thousands of years, it has never ceased to captivate and mystify us.",
    love_p9: "Love is not just a feeling; it is a choice. A choice to continue choosing this person, again and again. And what makes that choice possible is not hormones alone, but the unique bond built through shared time, mutual understanding, and love that was or will be created together.",
    love_unique: "The Uniqueness of Love",
    love_origin_p: "Over millions of years of survival and reproduction, genes favoring survival were retained while carriers of disadvantageous genes perished. Through continuous evolution, the neurons of romantic love first emerged in human ancestors about 4 million years ago. At that time, love’s function was to increase reproductive success, provide stable nurturing environments for offspring, promote cooperation and group cohesion, and enhance survival fitness. Yet, if that were all, how could love shape such richly diverse forms in modern life?",
    love_mechanism_p2: "When we are seen, respected, favored, and accepted by our beloved, dopamine, oxytocin, and vasopressin all rise, creating feelings of joy, warmth, peace, and sweetness. These feelings in turn drive us to see, respect, favor, and accept others—forming a positive feedback loop. Cycle after cycle, love emerges and flows between us.",
