    sorry_subtitle1: "I’m Sorry",
    sorry_subtitle2: "Forgiven",
    sorry_poem1_p1: "I’m sorry.",
    sorry_poem1_p2: "I hurt you.<br>I’m sorry.",
    sorry_poem1_p3: "It was my first time in this world,<br>my first time learning how to be human—<br>and yet, I hurt you.<br>I’m sorry.",
    sorry_poem1_p4: "To everyone I’ve ever hurt:<br>I’m sorry.",
    sorry_poem2_p1: "I’ve forgiven you.",
    sorry_poem2_p2: "For the hurt you caused me,<br>I’ve forgiven you.",
    sorry_poem2_p3: "It was your first time, too—<br>your first time in this world,<br>still finding your way,<br>still learning how to be human.",
    sorry_poem2_p4: "For the hurt you caused me,<br>I’ve forgiven you.",
    childhood_p1: "As a child, I didn’t understand this saying, because I stood on the receiving end of that scrutiny. In high school, I dismissed it as nonsense—a person’s life has countless variables; how could childhood alone seal a lifelong destiny?",
    childhood_p2: "The core of this proverb is never about predicting a child’s innate quality or life ceiling, but about revealing a simple essence: a child’s every word and deed, their temperament and character, are the combined result of their family, upbringing, and all social relations. It echoes Marx’s classic line: the human being is the sum of all social relations. A child’s image is precisely the composite snapshot of all their social relations. (The superstition of genetic determinism already belongs to the dust of the last century.)",
    childhood_p3: "Eating out today, watching relatives’ children in all their varied forms, I observed and reflected. I suddenly recalled my own childhood: there were always adults who looked at you with the same peculiar gaze I now possess—the name of that gaze is scrutiny. Scrutinizing whether you were worth the investment of their thoughts and guidance, whether their efforts on you would yield expected results rather than go to waste.",
    childhood_p4: "But to them, I was ultimately an outsider. An outsider can do very little: sometimes they can’t help but say an extra word, sometimes they are frustrated by your unfulfilled potential, and in the end they can only tell themselves not to meddle, detach, and withdraw. Now, I have become that outsider.",
    childhood_p5: "Standing on the other side of scrutiny, I finally understand: this scrutiny has nothing to do with gender, age, or education, nor is it the condescending privilege of elders. Its essence is foundational awareness—of child personality development, the psychology behind behavior, the shaping forces of family, environment, and society, and the boundaries of human interaction. It is the inner steadiness not to be swayed by a child’s emotions, the empathic ability to see through surface behavior to the child’s true needs, and the neutral awareness to observe without premature judgment.",
    childhood_p6: "Through a child’s behavior, one can often glimpse the emotional logic behind it, the ingrained behavioral patterns that sustain those emotions, and the foundational life mechanisms shaped by their growth environment, with family education at its core. These mechanisms lay down a profound life undertone in early childhood, becoming the most instinctive, subconscious responses when facing the world. Their influence runs through the entire arc of growth; reshaping and recalibrating them later is no easy feat.",
    childhood_p7: "This also attests to the old saying, “It is easier to move mountains than to change one’s nature.” Deeply reshaping these life mechanisms is essentially a thorough rewriting of one’s inherent cognition and instinctive reactions. This process demands enormous mental energy, sustained by long-term, resolute action, before it can truly succeed.",
    love_attachment_p1: "Attachment is an instinctual behavioral system with survival value, designed to protect the young from threat. Different caregiving strategies lead infants to form different attachment styles: secure, avoidant, and anxious-ambivalent. Securely attached adults display trust, stability, and positive expectations in love; avoidant adults tend to maintain emotional distance and feel discomfort with intimacy; anxious-ambivalent adults exhibit excessive preoccupation and insecurity in relationships.",
    love_attachment_p2: "Emotional resonance precedes physical attraction. The caregiver is life’s first template of love—we naturally gravitate toward those who resemble our caregivers in appearance, personality, expression, tone, gesture, and manner of relating. As we continue to fall for different people, the template updates. Neural regions encoding certain traits are reinforced, and when we encounter someone with those traits, that region activates, cascading into the love-related modules—hence love at first sight, or love that grows over time.",
    love_loss_p1: "The opposite of love is not hate, but pain. We are naturally drawn to the familiar and safe. When being seen, valued, favored, and accepted becomes habit, the brain designates the relationship as a “safe zone.” When it ends, we instinctively want to stay put, unable to break free from entrenched emotional patterns.",
    love_loss_p2: "Those with deep attachments are also those who persevere, who endure. Su Shi wrote: “Those who achieve great things in ancient times not only possessed extraordinary talent but also an indomitable will.” Most people lack this—they are ordinary. Pure rationality without regard for emotion is not the way. May this piece help you recognize love’s truth, and amidst reason and emotion, pain and love, find a middle path—without being trapped by attachment.",
    love_unique_p1: "Metaphysical beauty, when deconstructed by reason into tangible, concrete logic, loses its poetry.",
    love_unique_p2: "One can easily like anyone—it is merely hormonal instinct. One can also love all beings, needing only sufficient compassion.",
    love_unique_p3: "The uniqueness of love is the uniqueness of the person—rooted in the soul, sedimented through experience.",
    love_unique_p4: "Is anyone truly irreplaceable? From the physical perspective: genes can be cloned in twins, faces can resemble strangers on the street, and external traits like knowledge, ability, and wealth can all be matched or surpassed by others.",
    love_unique_p5: "Only the soul cannot be replicated. From the metaphysical view downward: from the moment of birth, every second is infinitely dynamic. Every choice you make, every thought you think, every firsthand experience—all the trajectories unique to your life interweave and accumulate, irreplicable by anyone, ultimately forging the one and only you 🫵",

